# IndieLicense integration

These files are ordinary source code owned by this app. There is no runtime
service, account, telemetry, or network call.

Generated configuration:

- Product: `{{PRODUCT}}`
- Distribution: {{DISTRIBUTION_DESCRIPTION}}
- Release build date: `{{BUILD_DATE}}` UTC
- Key persistence: tamper-evident files under `~/Library/Application Support/IndieLicense/`
- Denylist: {{DENYLIST_DESCRIPTION}}
- Keyless trial: {{TRIAL_DESCRIPTION}}
- Trial policy: {{TRIAL_POLICY_DESCRIPTION}}
- Purchase link: {{PURCHASE_DESCRIPTION}}
- Feedback link: {{FEEDBACK_DESCRIPTION}}
- SwiftUI activation view: {{UI_DESCRIPTION}}
- Development state harness: included in Debug builds, absent from Release

## Wire it into the app

1. Add every generated `.swift` file to the application target.
2. Create one long-lived `LicenseManager` at app startup.
3. Paid distribution: gate paid behavior with `license.hasFullAccess`
   (licensed, or inside the keyless trial when one is configured). Use
   `license.isLicensed` where a purchased key must be required regardless of
   trial state.
   Freemium distribution: the free core needs no license checks at all — never
   gate it. Gate only Pro features, with `license.isPro`. A user with no key is
   in the permanent `free` state, never `unlicensed`, and the app must stay
   fully usable there forever. Introducing Pro later must only ever lock *new*
   features; features that shipped free stay free.
4. Present your own key-entry UI, or `LicenseActivationView` when generated.
   With the SwiftUI files, drop `LicenseBadgeView(license: license)` into a
   toolbar or status area: it shows trial days remaining, "Trial ended",
   "Unlock", renewal, and failure states, opens the activation sheet on click,
   and disappears entirely once the app is licensed. The sheet shows a "Buy a
   license" button when `LicenseConfig.purchaseURL` is set.
   With the hard trial policy, additionally wrap the app's root view:
   `LicenseGateView(license: license) { MainAppView() }` — when the trial is
   over (or no valid key exists), the whole app is replaced by a
   non-dismissible lock screen with key entry and the purchase link. The
   `LicenseManager` powering the gate must live at app level, not inside a
   view that only appears after the gate.
5. Feedback, when configured. With `LicenseConfig.feedbackURL` the activation
   sheet shows a "Request a feature" link; consider also adding a Help-menu
   item that opens `license.feedbackLink()`. The link carries the current tier
   (free/pro/trial) and app version as query items and is only ever opened in
   the browser or mail client — no networking code exists in these modes.
   With `LicenseConfig.feedbackEndpoint` the sheet opens the generated
   `LicenseFeedbackView` instead (present it from a Help-menu item too), which
   POSTs the user's message as JSON exclusively when they press Send. That
   view is the app's only network call — never add background sends,
   telemetry, or analytics anywhere.
6. Keep checkout, prices, feature policy, copy, and localization in the app.
7. Update `LicenseConfig.buildDate` to the immutable UTC release day before
   every release. Never calculate it from `Date()` or file metadata.

`LicenseManager` revalidates the stored key during initialization and fails
closed on invalid configuration or inaccessible storage. `renewalRequired`
means the key still covers eligible older releases but not this newer build.

When `LicenseConfig.trialDays` is set, a customer with no stored key gets a
keyless trial: the start day is stamped once in the license file store on first launch,
the window is inclusive of its final day (same day math as trial keys), and
after it ends the state becomes `trialExpired`. Activating a real key ends the
trial. The trial is a convenience, not a security boundary — a determined user
can reset it, just as they could request another trial key.

## Test before shipping

- Valid key activates, persists, and remains active after relaunch.
- Malformed, tampered, and wrong-product keys stay locked.
- Trial expiry or update-window boundaries behave correctly for the key mode.
- With a keyless trial: full access during the window, `trialExpired` after,
  and the trial does not restart on relaunch.
- With the hard policy: the lock screen appears when access ends, cannot be
  dismissed, and activating a valid key immediately restores the app.
- Storage/configuration failures fail closed.
- A bundled signed denylist rejects a revoked key, when denylists are enabled.
- Every paid feature checks the same license state.
- Freemium: with no stored key the app reports `free`, every core feature
  works, nothing nags or expires, and a valid key flips `isPro` immediately.
- With a feedback URL: the "Request a feature" link opens the browser with the
  correct tier and version query items, and the app makes no network request.
- With a feedback endpoint: the sheet submits only on an explicit Send with
  the correct tier and version, handles offline/server errors without silent
  retries, and nothing else in the app performs a network request.

The private signing key and key-id state must remain outside the app repository.
Only the public key in `LicenseConfig.swift` belongs in the shipped app.

## Develop without resetting trials

Debug builds start with simulated full access (freemium: the free tier, the
state real users see) and automatically add an
**IndieLicense Testing** menu with license-state choices. Use it to inspect an active
trial, the final trial day, an ended trial, unlicensed, renewal, invalid-key,
storage-failure, and real persisted states. Simulated states live only in
memory and never read, write, or reset the real license store.

UI automation can select its initial state with
`--indielicense-state trial-expired` or the `INDIELICENSE_TEST_STATE`
environment value. Every preset raw value is accepted regardless of the
app's own model (useful for cross-model UI tests): freemium presets
`freemium-free`, `freemium-free-key-active`, `freemium-pro-not-active`,
`freemium-pro-lifetime`, `freemium-pro-updates`,
`freemium-pro-updates-expire-soon`, `freemium-pro-updates-lapsed`,
`freemium-pro-trial`, `freemium-pro-trial-last-day`,
`freemium-pro-trial-expired`; paid presets `full-access`, `licensed-updates`,
`licensed-updates-expire-soon`, `renewal-required`, `trial`,
`trial-ending-today`, `trial-expired`, `unlicensed`, `trial-key`,
`trial-key-expired`; shared `invalid-license`, `key-revoked`,
`storage-failure`, and `real`. Freemium presets also report
`simulatesProOnSale` so the app can preview its Pro-on-sale phase.

The harness uses only the standard Swift `DEBUG` compilation condition and
AppKit; it has no dependency on AppUnbound, Xcode launch schemes, or another
development tool. Define `INDIELICENSE_DISABLE_DEVELOPMENT_HARNESS` as an
additional Swift compilation condition to opt a Debug configuration out.
Release builds do not contain the scenario code, parser, or menu.
